orm another pung of Green Dragon, that would add to the score. But they only have one J2 in the hand. \n\nHmm. Maybe the user is aiming for a standard hand. \n\nIn conclusion, discarding B3 seems better because it allows keeping the W3 (for possible pung) and W7 (for chow). B3 is a middle tile, which might be less critical. Also, there are three B3s left, so the chance to get another is lower than W8 and W9. \n\nSo the best move is to play B3.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The best move is to discard B3 to preserve the potential for forming a pung of W3 or a chow with W7-8-9, while B3 is a middle tile with lower priority for key patterns.  \nReason: Discarding B3 maintains flexibility for future melds (e.g., W7-8-9 chow or W3 pung) and reduces risk of losing higher-value tiles. B3 has 3 remaining in the wall, making it less critical to retain.  \nAnswer: Play B3"}
